          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "How does XDR work?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "<p>An XDR platform is built on <a href=\"https://www.sekoia.io/en/integrations-catalog/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">an open, interoperable architecture that continuously collects security data from multiple sources across the enterprise</a>. This includes, but is not limited to, endpoints (EDR), networks (NDR), cloud environments, applications, databases, and more.</p>\n<p>All of this normalized data is then correlated and analyzed by the XDR platform using advanced techniques like machine learning. This process helps detect and prioritize suspicious activity and sophisticated threats that would otherwise go undetected with separate security tools.</p>\n<p>Once threats are identified, the XDR platform leverages orchestration, automation, response (SOAR) capabilities to guide analysts and quickly remediate incidents with pre-built playbooks and semi-automated response.</p>\n<p>This centralized operation around a single console provides SOC teams with complete visibility and a coordinated response to advanced, enterprise-wide cyber threats.</p>\n"
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What are the main differences between XDR, EDR, SIEM, and SOAR?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "<div class=\"et_pb_toggle et_pb_module et_pb_accordion_item et_pb_accordion_item_2 et_pb_toggle_open\">\n<div class=\"et_pb_toggle_content clearfix\">\n<p>Extended Detection and Response (XDR) distinguishes itself from other solutions by its unified approach to enterprise-wide threat detection and response.</p>\n<p>Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) focuses solely on monitoring and responding to threats at the endpoint/endpoint level.</p>\n<p>Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) primarily collects and correlates security logs and events, but does not cover incident response.</p>\n<p>Security Orchestration, Automation and Response (SOAR) automates security alert response processes by leveraging external data sources.</p>\n<p>Unlike these tools, an XDR platform integrates and analyzes data from multiple sources in real time: endpoints, networks, clouds, applications, databases, and more. This broad visibility allows them to detect sophisticated threats that would otherwise be missed by a point solution.</p>\n<p>XDR’s strength also lies in its capabilities to orchestrate, automate, and guide analysts for rapid and comprehensive incident response.</p>\n<p>Thus, the XDR approach consolidates the benefits of EDR, SIEM, and SOAR into a unified cybersecurity solution, providing maximum coverage.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n"
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Why is threat intelligence important?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "<div class=\"et_pb_toggle et_pb_module et_pb_accordion_item et_pb_accordion_item_4 et_pb_toggle_open\">\n<div class=\"et_pb_toggle_content clearfix\">\n<p>Threat intelligence is key to preventing cyberattacks. By providing intelligence through a CTI platform, it helps identify threats in advance. This makes it possible to take defensive countermeasures and detect attacks in real-time. For SOCs (<a href=\"https://www.sekoia.io/en/homepage/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><span id=\"tgtAlignment_168\" class=\"ts-alignment-element\" data-is-focusable=\"true\">Security</span> <span id=\"tgtAlignment_167\" class=\"ts-alignment-element\" data-is-focusable=\"true\">Operation C</span><span id=\"tgtAlignment_169\" class=\"ts-alignment-element\" data-is-focusable=\"true\">enters</span></a>), it saves time and gives them peace of mind in risk management.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n"
            }
          }
        ]
